From: payerle@alexander.physics.umd.edu (Tom Payerle)
Subject: How to get a script executed at shutdown?
Date: 1997/08/08
Message-ID: <5sg0fh$hos$1@hecate.umd.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 262942049
Keywords: DU 4.0 Digital Unix shutdown
Organization: Physics Department, University of Maryland
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.misc


I am interested in having certain machine-specific commands be executed when
the machine (AS500 5/333) is shutdown.  I have tried adding files of the
sort /sbin/rc0.d/K99mystuff, and it successfully executes on an
init 0, but apparently shutdown -h now and shutdown -r now do not actually
do an init 0.  (Man pages suggest that shutdown now [going to single user mode]
will, haven't verified.  Also states that -h and -r options do /sbin/halt and
/sbin/reboot respectively, and no mention of init 0 in their man pages.)
Therefore stuff in rc0.d are not being executed.

I can do a hack by renaming /sbin/shutdown to /sbin/shutdown.orig and making
my own /sbin/shutdown script which ends by calling shutdown.orig, but this
would either require some serious programming or loss of functionality.  (Eg,
handling shutdown -h now is easy, but shutdown -h +500 is quite a bit harder,
as I don't want my script running 500 minutes before the shutdown occurs.)

Has anyone successfully dealt with this problem?  Email responses (preferred)
will be summarized to the group.


