David,

The SCSI tape devices are documented under "man tz".  (Go figger.)
You might want to also read the man pages for mtio and mt.

I recommend you build the "whatis" database with the command "catman
-w".  This indexes your man pages, and then you can find relevant man
pages with (for example) "apropos tape" or "man -k tape").  Note:
catman can take quite some time to complete and will use up some disk
space.  (On my OSF 3.2D system, it is ~250k).

Your intuition about these being for the rewinding/non-rewinding (with
our with out the preceding "n") and for "high", "low" and "medium"
densities is correct.  However, not all device names are relevant to
all types of tape drives (some don't support more than one or two
densities), and there are other restrictions (e.g. you can't switch
density in the middle of a tape on most types of drives.) The man page
should cover these situations.

Good luck,

-rob

